<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mustansirite Hardship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1064–1071</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mustansirite Hardship was a prolonged crisis in Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1064 to 1071, caused by seven consecutive years of catastrophically low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> floods. According to the site's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> profile, it killed an estimated 40,000 people, halved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>'s population and broke the army into warring factions. Named after the reigning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mustansir, it was a major turning point in the decline of the Fatimid Caliphate.</p></div>
